%% Sentinel OMS — compound failure scenario (the hardest end-to-end test)
%% Author: Yashvardhan Gaur
%% Timeout + hidden acceptance + fills + cancel race + crash + restart + reconciliation
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client
  participant O as OMS core
  participant L as Event ledger (Postgres)
  participant B as Broker (simulated)
  participant R as Reconciler

  C->>O: place order — 4 contracts (idempotency key K)
  O->>L: persist intent — CREATED (before any submission)
  O->>B: submit (client order id K)
  Note over O,B: response lost — timeout, NO broker order id
  O->>L: SUBMITTING -> UNKNOWN (instrument locked, no blind retry)
  Note over B: broker actually ACCEPTED the order

  R->>B: query open orders by client id K
  B-->>R: found — WORKING, broker id X
  R->>L: UNKNOWN -> WORKING (order recovered, not resubmitted)

  B-->>O: partial fill +1 (1 of 4)
  O->>L: PARTIAL — position 1
  B-->>O: partial fill +1 (2 of 4)
  O->>L: PARTIAL — position 2

  C->>O: cancel remaining
  O->>B: cancel (broker id X)
  O->>L: CANCEL_PENDING
  B-->>O: fill +1 DURING cancel-pending (3 of 4)
  O->>L: fill recorded — position 3

  Note over O,L: CRASH — process killed mid-flight
  Note over O: restart
  O->>L: rebuild orders, fills, locks, authorized risk from ledger
  R->>B: reconcile — broker says 3 filled, remainder canceled
  R->>L: CANCELED (remaining 1) — position converged = 3

  Note over O: assertions — no duplicate entry ·<br/>remaining quantity correct · no over-exit
  O->>B: protective exit restored for exactly 3 contracts
